  "title": "Fort Custer, Montana",
  "cell": "c8/c85",
  "geometryBasis": "The federal survey’s 600-foot bar, north arrow and individual footprints set the parade ground and building positions relative to each other. The USGS historical site vicinity and modern 3DEP bluff relief constrain a best-fit location.",
  "sectionBasis": "E. F. Everitt’s published stereo cards establish the gabled officers’ houses with porches and chimneys, and the broad hospital with a hipped two-story center, open wraparound verandah, rooftop belvedere and lower gabled wings.",
  "limitations": [
    "Fort Custer is represented in the late 19th century. The local museum records the post in 1877–1898 and confirms its buildings are gone. The historical placement is excluded from the current-world view.",
    "Individual relative footprints are traced from the federal survey; the absolute site alignment is approximate. Modern, independently maintained terrain supplies the site relief. The outlying stable, storage and service groups are outside this exterior reconstruction.",
    "The two historical photos are sepia. Colors, unphotographed sides, heights and roof details are interpreted. The four barracks use the documented measured plan and simplified period construction forms."
  ],
